{"title":"2024 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 12","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1_(Pure_Logic)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1 (Pure Logic)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2_(Clever_intuition)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2 (Clever intuition)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_4_(Rigorous)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4 (Rigorous)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_1_by_Pi_Academy_(Fast_and_Easy_⚡🚀)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 1 by Pi Academy (Fast and Easy ⚡🚀)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_2_by_SpreadTheMathLove\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 2 by SpreadTheMathLove</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_3_by_TheBeautyofMath\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 3 by TheBeautyofMath</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A group of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/9/e59e2c6e83eb78cca610a5fd4070ae01c8d4ae60.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$100$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" /> students from different countries meet at a mathematics competition.\nEach student speaks the same number of languages, and, for every pair of\nstudents <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />, student <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> speaks some language that student <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> does not speak,\nand student <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> speaks some language that student <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> does not speak. What is the\nleast possible total number of languages spoken by all the students?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/6/d/d6d8b631f3da4a6703a3374551c52588fd44cc4f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A) } 9 \\qquad\\textbf{(B) } 10 \\qquad\\textbf{(C) } 12 \\qquad\\textbf{(D) } 51 \\qquad\\textbf{(E) } 100$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"401\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_1_.28Pure_Logic.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1_(Pure_Logic)\">Solution 1 (Pure Logic)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1 (Pure Logic)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We think of the languages in this problem as boxes. First, we start with the first choice: 9. We see that we can arrange the groups of people into the 9 boxes. We take 9 people of different languages and arrange them in each of the boxes. This means we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/a/75a6e3057c8772b47b700430a90e889688305562.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$100 - 9 \\times 9 = 19$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"134\" height=\"15\" /> people remaining. We then take 18 people of different language (as they can be put in a pair and still qualify) and put them in the boxes. This gives us 1 person left over. Now, this is where MAA wants you to choose 10, but one can quickly see that the last person can be put into any group of three (except a group that includes their language) and still qualify whilst maintaining all the constraints. Therefore our answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/d/0/bd092b73eb02ad34cd3d767d4f347db11e312ebf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(A) } 9}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"56\" height=\"28\" />.\n</p><p>This solution is a basic introduction/summary of the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Pigeonhole_Principle\" title=\"Pigeonhole Principle\">Pigeonhole Principle</a>\n</p><p>~Pinotation\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_.28Clever_intuition.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(Clever_intuition)\">Solution 2 (Clever intuition)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (Clever intuition)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>There are 100 students at the mathematics convention.\n</p><p>For any pair of students <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> we see that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/9/1e9c5214d97a0765b9ab428a96aa1865b0a75df5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB \\in \\{X,Y\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"109\" height=\"20\" /> with WLOG, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/8/158fccfef8b9c1a61a13ba579c3a32ec090c9cb7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(X, Y)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"54\" height=\"20\" /> being a unique language individual to either <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>In other words, either student <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> or student <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> received language <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> or language <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/5/ce58e4af225c93d08606c26554caaa5ae32edeba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, in which the order they receive their language is unimportant. \n</p><p>Given this the number of languages must divide the number of students evenly in order to achieve unique <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/c/81cee936d2982eea5fb6e68b6167da70a1c690fb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X,Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"37\" height=\"16\" /> pairs that satisfy the conditions stated in the problem.\n</p><p>The question now is \"What is the number of languages that divide the number of students?\", which is equivalent to asking \"How many numbers divide 100?\", in which we use the number of factors theorem of the prime factorization of 100, being <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/b/0/fb0800e0b46d21d44649bae12b26cc2883e2f7c4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^2 \\times 5^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"57\" height=\"18\" />, and the number of factors being <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/3/8/238cd989585087e85e5575d072047104c30b85d5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2+1)(2+1) = (3)(3) = 9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"215\" height=\"20\" />.\n</p><p>Therefore our answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/d/0/bd092b73eb02ad34cd3d767d4f347db11e312ebf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(A) } 9}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"56\" height=\"28\" />.\n</p><p>~Pinotation\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let's say we have some number of languages. Then each student will speak some amount of those languages, and no two people can have the same combination of languages or else the conditions will no longer be satisfied. Notice that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/4/6647f5e9540e32a132ada0f6a148e9838b785af1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dbinom94=126\\geq100$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"142\" height=\"55\" />. So each of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/9/e59e2c6e83eb78cca610a5fd4070ae01c8d4ae60.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$100$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" /> students can speak some <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/2/bf2c9074b396e3af0dea52d792660eea1c77f10f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> languages. Thus, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/4/374ad4e9adc96666cde62dd31911b54d38e83c56.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{9}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"20\" height=\"23\" /> is our answer.\n</p><p>~lprado\n~LaTeX edits by Elephant200\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_4_.28Rigorous.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4_(Rigorous)\">Solution 4 (Rigorous)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4 (Rigorous)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> be the total number of languages spoken by all the students, and let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> be the number of languages that each student speaks.\nSince all students speak the same number of languages, the condition given in the question can be modified as -\n</p><p>\"If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> is the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />-element subset containing the union of the languages spoken by all students, each of the 100 students speaks a different (unique) <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />-element subset of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> combination of languages.\"\n</p><p>In more mathematical terms, this means <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/2/7e294bcf012fbe1c2ebafdd60764f28877525d05.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${n \\choose k} \\ge 100$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"92\" height=\"55\" />. (Using <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Pigeonhole_Principle\" title=\"Pigeonhole Principle\">PHP</a>)\n</p><p>Because we need the least value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> must be the closest integer to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/7/f275b1f33e3c37ece0e41964f0bcb806e95b46b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{n}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"13\" height=\"33\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/5/0d554ce99784287d1c075a355d8bf86a830699f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Rightarrow {n \\choose \\frac{n}{2}} \\ge 100$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"117\" height=\"55\" /> for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/e/ffe388d3a177b46bbb5eb8b642b423091e6ab74f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n = 2m$\" width=\"59\" height=\"12\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/2/822cec0b4b214bf06ea2315ac7f83f010ae84c87.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${n \\choose \\frac{n-1}{2}} \\ge 100$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"111\" height=\"55\" /> for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/2/c/72c5e5eca42e4a07a4fe92d17bd1a4dcb6b4582c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=2m-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"93\" height=\"15\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/1/f21b49d4a1d07db0896300cd7c894fdbb50883a6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m \\in \\mathbb{N}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"50\" height=\"13\" />\n</p><p>In the context of this question, it will be fastest to just substitute <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/6/586ac3c14ea1f30a5e0de57c686a3ab323959f74.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\normalsize n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> to each of the option values to find the least value possible as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/d/0/bd092b73eb02ad34cd3d767d4f347db11e312ebf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(A) } 9}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"56\" height=\"28\" />\n</p><p>For a more rigorous proof, where options are not an option, one can manually count up from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/d/9/1d96ecc15129c5631ba4a2c6578e91e3d13e3baf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" /> as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/9/e59e2c6e83eb78cca610a5fd4070ae01c8d4ae60.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$100$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" /> is a small number. \n</p><p>~laythe_enjoyer211\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_1_by_Pi_Academy_.28Fast_and_Easy_.E2.9A.A1.F0.9F.9A.80.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_1_by_Pi_Academy_(Fast_and_Easy_⚡🚀)\">Video Solution 1 by Pi Academy (Fast and Easy ⚡🚀)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 1 by Pi Academy (Fast and Easy ⚡🚀)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/YqKmvSR1Ckk?feature=shared\">https://youtu.be/YqKmvSR1Ckk?feature=shared</a>\n</p><p>~ Pi Academy\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_2_by_SpreadTheMathLove\">Video Solution 2 by SpreadTheMathLove</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 2 by SpreadTheMathLove\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N46YcQPc4ro&amp;t=0s\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N46YcQPc4ro&amp;t=0s</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_3_by_TheBeautyofMath\">Video Solution 3 by TheBeautyofMath</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 3 by TheBeautyofMath\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/dfF39udgqc8?t=274\">https://youtu.be/dfF39udgqc8?t=274</a> in Rapid Fire\n</p><p><br />\n~IceMatrix\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10B\" title=\"2024 AMC 10B\">2024 AMC 10B</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10B_Problems\" title=\"2024 AMC 10B Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10B_Answer_Key\" title=\"2024 AMC 10B Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c13\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_11\" title=\"2024 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 11\">Problem 11</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_13\" title=\"